  "drivablePrefix": 1,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:1c31f9536ca2a7dd",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C56-si-tuviera",
      "sequence": 2275,
      "title": "si tuviera",
      "headword": "si tuviera",
      "romanization": "si tuviera",
      "gloss": "if I had time — supposing something that is not true, which is the whole reason the last ten chapters happened",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:6436a52971e60580",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the real one. (Si tengo tiempo, hablo español.) Now the supposing form of tener. (Tuviera.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Put them together and you can talk about a day you are not having."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the world that is not the case",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "si + supposing form, then the -ría form."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                ""
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "Si tengo tiempo, hablo español., and I might well have it.",
                "Si tuviera tiempo, hablaría español., but I do not."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The second sentence says two things at once: what would happen, and that it is not happening. That is carried entirely by the verb forms — no extra word is needed to say but I don't."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both halves change together. Si takes the supposing form; the result half takes the -ría form you learned for would. Neither half can be left in the present."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And si never takes the present subjunctive. Not si tenga — that combination simply does not occur in Spanish. The supposing form after si is always the -ra one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is worth knowing why the pairing feels lopsided. The si half is not asserting anything, so it uses the form Spanish reserves for the unasserted — and the result half is making a claim, just a claim about a world that does not exist, which is what -ría is for. Two different jobs, two different forms, in one sentence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
